🔄 What are Iterators?
Iterators in Java are used to traverse (loop through) collections like List, Set, Map, etc.

🚨 Fail-Fast Iterator
Throws ConcurrentModificationException if the collection is modified structurally while iterating (except via iterator itself).

Detects changes using a modCount (modification count).

Not thread-safe.

✅ Example (Fail-Fast — ArrayList, HashMap, etc.)
java
Copy
Edit
List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
list.add("A");
list.add("B");

for (String item : list) {
    list.remove(0);  // 🔥 Throws ConcurrentModificationException
}
🛡️ Fail-Safe Iterator
Does not throw exception if the collection is modified during iteration.

Works on a cloned copy of the collection (snapshot).

Thread-safe, but changes during iteration may not be visible.

✅ Example (Fail-Safe — CopyOnWriteArrayList, ConcurrentHashMap)
java
Copy
Edit
CopyOnWriteArrayList<String> list = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();
list.add("A");
list.add("B");

for (String item : list) {
    list.remove(item);  // ✅ No exception, safe to modify
}
🧩 Key Differences: Fail-Fast vs Fail-Safe
| Feature                     | **Fail-Fast**                             | **Fail-Safe**                               |
| --------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Throws exception on modify? | ✅ Yes (`ConcurrentModificationException`) | ❌ No                                        |
| Works on live collection?   | ✅ Yes                                     | ❌ Works on a **copy**                       |
| Thread-safe?                | ❌ No                                      | ✅ Yes                                       |
| Performance                 | ✅ Faster                                  | ❌ Slower (copies the collection)            |
| Examples                    | `ArrayList`, `HashMap`, `HashSet`         | `CopyOnWriteArrayList`, `ConcurrentHashMap` |


🧠 Summary
Use Fail-Fast when:

You want to catch concurrent modifications during iteration.

You’re working in a single-threaded environment.

Use Fail-Safe when:

You need thread-safe iteration.

You’re okay with a slight performance trade-off.